rel eats the cow.**.\n   - This indicates that the squirrel has eaten the cow.\n\n10.**Squirrel visits the rabbit.**.\n    - This indicates that the squirrel has visited the rabbit.\n\n11.**If someone visits the squirrel and the squirrel chases the cow then the cow is round.**.\n    - This is not directly relevant to the bear and rabbit relationship.\n\n12.**If someone is big and red then they chase the rabbit.**.\n    - This is not directly relevant to the bear and rabbit relationship.\n\n13.If no one visits the rabbit then the rabbit doesn\'t visit the bear.\n    - This is completely relevant to the bear and rabbit relationship.\n\n14.**If the squirrel ignores the cow and the cow is square then the cow is old.**.\n    - This is completely relevant to the bear and rabbit relationship.\n\n15.**If someone feeds the cow and the cow feeds the rabbit then the cow runs away from the rabbit.**.\n    - This is completely relevant to the bear and rabbit relationship.\n\n16.**If the squirrel ignores the cow and the cow is square then the cow is old.**.\n    - This is completely relevant to the bear and rabbit relationship.\n\n17.**If someone ignores the squirrel then the squirrel feeds the rabbit.**.\n    - This is completely relevant to the bear and rabbit relationship.\n\n18.**If someone feeds the bear then they run away from the cow.**.\n    - This is completely relevant to the bear and rabbit relationship.\n\n### Conclusion:.\nFrom the given information, there is no statement that directly or indirectly indicates that the bear visits the rabbit. The bear and rabbit are only mentioned in the context of the squirrel and the cow, and there is no information suggesting a direct relationship between the bear and the rabbit.\n\nTherefore, based on the provided context, the statement "The bear visits the rabbit" is **known**\n. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
